

I have a query to retrieve a list of sales of a particular coffee-maker. It is running very slowly. What might be wrong? What might I be able to do to speed it up?

```
SELECT *
FROM Sales AS s
INNER JOIN SalesItems AS si ON s.ID = si.SaleID
LEFT JOIN Employees AS e ON s.EmployeeName = e.Name
WHERE si.ItemSku = ‘CFE01’;
```

TABLES:
Sales - ID, CustomerID, EmployeeID, EmployeeName, TotalAmount, PurchasedDate, ...
SalesItems - ID, SaleID, ItemSku, ItemQuantity, ItemAmount, ...
Employees - ID, Name, Phone, Title, ...

ANSWER:
* We have EmployeeName in Sales table as well. Why do we have EmployeeName here when we already have EmployeeId here. And we are making join on the basis of EmployeeName instead of EmployeeId. Is this what we really want? What all fields we need from Employees table?
* Why are we selecting ALL columns across all tables. What columns do we really want?
* Do we have indexes in the table? To make this query faster, we should add indexes on SaleId column of SalesItems table, EmployeeName column of Sales table, Name column of Employees table and ItemSku column of SalesItems table.

